{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md | head -140; git remote -v'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01;\n# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\n# (owner ruling 2026-11-07; see Gate Ladder G0).\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n\n```sh\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n```\n\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\n\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\n\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\norigin\t/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld (fetch)\norigin\t/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld (push)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='collab/orch-lane/1794400000000-architect-eval-bridge-design.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log'\nfor l in open(p):\n try:\n  x=json.loads(l); i=x.get('item',{});\n  if i.get('type')=='agent_message': print(i.get('text',''))\n except: pass\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='collab/orch-lane/1794400000000-architect-eval-bridge-design.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log'\nfor l in open(p):\n try:\n  x=json.loads(l); i=x.get('item',{});\n  if i.get('type')=='agent_message': print(i.get('text',''))\n except: pass\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"I’ll read the architecture rules, eval refusal, compiler pipeline, and safepoint code, then draft a read-only design with file-and-line citations and an implementation plan.\nThe existing dynamic-scope model gives eval a useful starting point: functions with eval are marked for a heap scope, and included units already accept the caller’s `%scope` and `%ctx`. I’m checking how to reuse that path without reinitializing PHP state or resetting the run’s safepoint counter.\nTwo details need to be explicit in the design. Eval’s normal result is null, while the include wrapper maps fall-through to `1`. Also, a function with no statically known variables currently skips scope cleanup; eval can create variables in that function, so the implementation must cover that lifecycle case.\nThe design will keep parsing outside the VM: eval submits a compilation request, waits for an artifact, then calls its entry in the caller’s task. Node and Dart can use a configured compiler service; without one, eval keeps the pinned refusal. I also found a scope-analysis gap to address: the grammar has a dedicated `Eval` expression, but the current dynamic-scope check recognizes eval through function-call names.\nOne more gap belongs in the implementation plan: `try-require` returns a module name, not an export callable. I’ll specify a small, profiled accessor for an already installed export to complete the handoff. I’ll also keep redeclaration timing, method context, eval-local statics, and diagnostic locations pending until the PHP 8.3.35 oracle pins them.\nThe bridge should compile eval source through a composition-supplied compiler service, then execute the resulting entry **in the caller’s task, against the caller’s existing variable cells and run budget**. Parsing remains outside the VM. Node and Dart need a configured service; without one, eval retains the pinned refusal.\n\nThis is a proposed design, not an implementation report. No files were edited or tests run.\n\n**1. Compilation path and host policy**\n\nThe current refusal has two paths: the `Eval` prefix is listed in `prefix-refusals`, and the plain-call path explicitly refuses `\"eval\"`. The pinned diagnostic is a map containing `:yang.php/diagnostic :yang.php/unsupported`, `:construct :eval`, and the source rule/span. See [lower.cljc:100](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:100), [lower.cljc:1307](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1307), [lower.cljc:1861](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1861), and [core/lower.cljc:17](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/lower.cljc:17).\n\nReplace the supported eval expression with this conceptual sequence:\n\n```clojure\n(let [source <evaluate operand once>]\n  (php/eval-run source caller-scope caller-context run-context))\n```\n\n`php/eval-run` submits a compilation request and waits for its correlated outcome using ordinary stream effects. It must never call `cst/parse-source`, `lower-program`, or a host PHP evaluator directly. This follows the existing requirement that portable code requests compilation through streams and that the coordinator answers those requests. See [yang.antlr.md:868](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:868).\n\nThe proposed path is:\n\n```text\ncaller evaluates source operand\n  → compilation request stream\n  → PHP parser worker, eval source goal\n  → CST validation and scope analysis\n  → PHP lowering, eval-unit entry\n  → canonical source envelope\n  → active safepoint derivation\n  → publication and ordinary linker admission\n  → installed entry called in caller task\n```\n\nReuse the normal parser and lowering machinery, with an explicit **eval-unit emission mode**. Do not invoke `lower-program` unchanged: it wraps the body in `php/run-main`, and its linked form calls `php/init!`. `php/run-main` resets output and dynamic handler context; that would break the surrounding execution. See [lower.cljc:3759](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3759) and [prelude.cljc:5070](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5070).\n\nThe eval emitter should reuse `context`, `unit-body`, and the unit return escape, producing an entry conceptually shaped as:\n\n```clojure\n(fn [%runtime %scope %ctx %hooks]\n  <eval unit>)\n```\n\nThe existing included-unit emitter already demonstrates `%scope`/`%ctx` arguments. Its return sentinel is also reusable, but eval must map fall-through to `:php/null`; the include wrapper currently maps it to `1`. See [lower.cljc:3648](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3648), [lower.cljc:3721](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3721), [lower.cljc:3840](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3840), and [prelude_module.cljc:84](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc:84).\n\nThree details complete this path:\n\n- **Eval source goal.** Start the existing lexer in PHP mode, retaining its default mode beneath it so `?>` can leave PHP mode. Do not prepend an opening tag and then repair offsets. The grammar already has PHP mode and mixed HTML parsing; Zend likewise compiles eval after the opening-tag position. Preserve original eval-byte spans and line starts. See [PhpLexer.g4:117](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/antlr/php/PhpLexer.g4:117), [PhpParser.g4:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/antlr/php/PhpParser.g4:38), [cst.clj:41](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/yang/php/antlr/cst.clj:41), and [PHP 8.3.35 zend_execute.c:4630](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/php-8.3.35/Zend/zend_execute.c#L4630).\n- **Runtime imports.** `%runtime` supplies the caller’s actual PHP runtime callable values. Bind the eval tree’s free `php/` names from that environment; do not instantiate another PHP runtime inside the installed artifact. Generate the environment from the existing definition inventory rather than maintaining another list. This supports bundled and linked callers without duplicating their globals, function tables, or class tables. The lowering already avoids naming `php.rt/*`, and the prelude has one definition inventory. See [lower.cljc:33](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:33), [prelude.cljc:32](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:32), and [prelude.cljc:5302](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5302).\n- **Entry retrieval.** Add a small profiled `code/entry` effect that retrieves a declared export from an **already installed, manifest-pinned module**. This is necessary: `try-require` returns a module name, not its exports. The accessor checks the installed manifest address and export name, then returns the receiver’s lowered binding. It neither parses nor installs code. Implement it through the existing effect-handler registry; no PHP-specific VM opcode or evaluator branch is needed. See [engine.cljc:1168](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1168), [module.cljc:40](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:40), [module.cljc:153](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:153), and [engine.cljc:2737](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2737).\n\nThe compilation request carries source bytes, request identity, frontend/compiler revisions, eval goal, source provenance, and compilation options. **It carries no caller cells, values, continuation, or host handles.** Those stay with the suspended caller and become arguments only after installation. Source envelopes must preserve frontend metadata so the normal safepoint pass can find eval’s sites. See [yang.antlr.md:856](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:856), [core/stage.cljc:25](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/stage.cljc:25), and [core/lower.cljc:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/lower.cljc:76).\n\nHost policy:\n\n| Composition | Proposed behavior |\n|---|---|\n| JVM with configured compiler worker | Compile at runtime through the PHP worker. |\n| Node/Dart with configured compiler service | Submit the same request; receive portable artifacts and execute locally. |\n| Any host without a configured compiler | Refuse eval with the pinned `:yang.php/unsupported`, `:construct :eval` diagnostic. |\n| Parserless host holding only the outer program | Still refuse; precompiling the outer program does not compile a future runtime string. |\n\nThe current PHP parser is explicitly JVM-only. Service-backed compilation is already an architectural alternative, distinct from offline local compilation. See [cst.clj:1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/yang/php/antlr/cst.clj:1) and [yang.antlr.md:493](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:493).\n\nPrecompiled eval artifacts may later satisfy the same request by an exact source/profile/provenance key. They must execute at the eval statement every time; artifact reuse must not become include-once behavior. This is optional caching, not the first implementation slice.\n\n**2. Exact symbol sharing, ownership, and budget**\n\nUse **one dictionary of variable-cell identities**, not a dictionary of copied values.\n\nFor a function containing eval, allocate its normal CV cells and adopt those exact cells into `%scope` before the body runs. Eval’s top-level `$x` lowers through `php/dict-*`; the caller’s ordinary `$x` may continue using its lexical CV. Both paths reach the same cell. A name introduced only by eval gets a cell through `php/scope-cell`. Existing dictionary operations already preserve reference markers and delegate assignment/unset to the local-cell operations. See [lower.cljc:3188](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3188), [prelude_module.cljc:129](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc:129), and [prelude.cljc:2158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2158).\n\nConsequently:\n\n```text\ncaller CV $x ───────────────┐\n                           ├── same task-local cell\neval scope[\"x\"] ────────────┘\n```\n\nThere is no copy-back phase, including on exceptions. Writes, reference rebinding, and unset become visible immediately. For global eval, pass `:php/globals`; the dictionary operations already dispatch that sentinel to the existing global operations. See [prelude_module.cljc:154](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc:154). This matches Zend’s use of the caller’s existing symbol table, rebuilding its CV projection when necessary. See [PHP 8.3.35 zend_vm_def.h:6068](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/php-8.3.35/Zend/zend_vm_def.h#L6068).\n\nTwo implementation corrections are required:\n\n- **Recognize the actual Eval expression.** Scope analysis currently recognizes `\"eval\"` through `functionCallName`, while the grammar also defines a dedicated `Eval '(' expression ')'` alternative. Mark that expression’s owning scope dynamic without descending into nested function scopes. Test it directly; do not infer coverage from `\"eval\"` appearing in `dynamic-call?`. See [scope.cljc:113](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/scope.cljc:113), [scope.cljc:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/scope.cljc:193), and [PhpParser.g4:500](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/antlr/php/PhpParser.g4:500).\n- **Release the complete dynamic scope.** Ordinary function exit must release `%scope` even when the static CV list is empty. Eval may create the function’s first variable. Generator cleanup must likewise release its dynamic dictionary, including eval-created cells, rather than only the original CV vector. Eval itself borrows this scope and must not release it on return. See [lower.cljc:3157](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3157), [lower.cljc:3191](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3191), and [prelude_module.cljc:146](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc:146).\n\nThis sharing stays inside one task. The compiler service receives syntax; the linker installs code; the caller applies that code to its own cells. It introduces no shared mutable heap between tasks, consistent with [datom.world.md:24](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:24).\n\n**Eval execution counts against the caller’s P7-a budget.** Mark the eval invocation as one `:call` site, after operand evaluation and before submitting compilation. Derive all eval-unit loop/call sites, including sites in functions declared by eval. Supply the caller’s active hook closures through `%hooks`, using local hook aliases in the derivation profile. Do not run `safepoint/program` for the eval unit: that function creates a new counter. See [safepoint.cljc:9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:9), [safepoint.cljc:23](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:23), [safepoint.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:38), and [yang/safepoint.cljc:96](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:96).\n\nUnbounded runs supply no-op budget hooks. Nested eval and later calls to eval-declared functions retain the same run hooks. Compiler parsing, hashing, transport waits, and publication use separate compiler limits; they are not PHP safepoint units. P7-a explicitly bounds execution between hooks rather than wall time or blocking host operations. See [yang.php.md:1928](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:1928).\n\n**3. Scope and error decisions**\n\n| Case | Proposed rule |\n|---|---|\n| Nested eval | A new correlated compilation request; reuse the current scope and run context. Each eval has its own return escape. |\n| Eval inside a function | Share that invocation’s dictionary, including parameters, statics already bound into CVs, and reference captures. Do not expose another invocation’s locals. |\n| Global eval | Use `:php/globals`; ordinary assignments and `$GLOBALS` element operations reach the same global state. |\n| Named functions/classes declared by eval | Register in the request’s normal declaration tables. They survive eval return and function return. Named function bodies get their normal function scope, not implicit capture of the eval caller’s locals. |\n| Hoisting | Occurs when that eval unit executes, never while compiling the outer program. Reuse the unit’s function/class ordering, subject to the redeclaration correction below. |\n| Methods/closures calling eval | Pass `$this`, lexical class scope, and called-class context explicitly; sharing variable scope alone is insufficient. |\n| Namespace/import environment | Treat eval as a separate compilation unit; do not copy the caller’s `namespace`/`use` environment. Explicit declarations in eval establish its own environment. |\n| Top-level eval `return` | Return from that eval only. Fall-through and bare `return` yield PHP null. |\n\nThe existing scope, declaration, hoisting, and included-context mechanisms are at [yang.php.md:179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:179), [lower.cljc:3075](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3075), [lower.cljc:3682](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3682), [prelude.cljc:2293](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2293), and [prelude.cljc:4120](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4120). PHP’s eval contract establishes inherited variable scope, separate-unit compilation, persistent variable changes, and null/explicit-return results; Zend additionally preserves method scope and `$this`. See the [PHP eval manual](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.eval.php) and [zend_vm_def.h:6068](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/php-8.3.35/Zend/zend_vm_def.h#L6068).\n\nDo not blindly inherit the current class hoister’s “class already exists, skip” behavior. The eval path must distinguish “hoisted earlier in **this execution**” from “declared by an earlier eval.” The latter must not silently suppress a redeclaration. Pin exact fatal timing and catchability before changing the shared helper. See [prelude.cljc:3338](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:3338).\n\nFailure classification:\n\n- Invalid eval syntax becomes guest `ParseError`, with no eval statements executed. Add `CompileError extends Error` and `ParseError extends CompileError`; neither is in the current seed table. See [prelude.cljc:5114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5114) and the [PHP eval manual](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.eval.php).\n- Unsupported profile constructs retain their compiler diagnostics. Missing compiler/parser, worker failure, cancellation, resource limits, and link/admission failures remain operational outcomes, outside guest `Throwable` handling. Preserve their structured refusal in the run result instead of inventing a PHP exception or returning false. See [yang.antlr.md:4397](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:4397) and [yang.php.md:1948](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:1948).\n- Runtime guest exceptions use the existing caller handler stack. Mutations already made remain visible; eval introduces no transaction or rollback. The existing handler machinery is at [prelude.cljc:163](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:163).\n\nEval-local `static` declarations, strict-types details, magic constants, and coercion of non-string operands require oracle fixtures. Do not bind an eval-unit static declaration to the caller function’s persistent static table merely because eval shares its variables. The current unit-level static lowering is refused, so supporting it is a separate, explicit scope decision. See [lower.cljc:2878](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:2878).\n\n**4. Acceptance fixtures**\n\nAdd a distinct eval corpus group for §3.12 and P9’s symbol-table acceptance. Keep hand-derived expectations separate from oracle evidence: every unrecorded fixture remains `:oracle :pending`, including those with clear expected output. That follows the current P9 fixture convention; claimed behavior must be oracle-confirmed before landing. See [programs_p9.cljc:6](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p9.cljc:6), [yang.php.md:2120](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:2120), and [yang.php.md:2436](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:2436).\n\nThe following are hand-derived expectations. Sources below omit the outer `<?php`.\n\n| Fixture | Source or precise case | Expected stdout |\n|---|---|---|\n| Global read/write/create | `$x=1; eval('$x=2; $y=3;'); echo $GLOBALS['x'],$y;` | `23` |\n| Function local scope | `$x=90; function f(){ $x=2; eval('$x+=3; $y=7;'); return $x+$y; } echo f(),':',$x;` | `12:90` |\n| Nested eval | `$x=1; $inner='$x+=2;'; eval('eval($inner); $x*=3;'); echo $x;` | `9` |\n| Existing reference | `$a=1; $b=&$a; eval('$a=4;'); echo $a,$b;` | `44` |\n| Eval-created reference | `$x=1; eval('$y =& $x;'); $y=9; echo $x;` | `9` |\n| Unset and recreate | `$x=1; eval('unset($x);'); echo isset($x)?1:0; eval('$x=2;'); echo $x;` | `02` |\n| Explicit global in local eval | `$g=1; function f(){ eval('global $g; $g=5;'); echo $g; } f(); echo $g;` | `55` |\n| Fall-through result | `$r=eval('$x=1;'); echo $r===null?'N':'X'; echo $x;` | `N1` |\n| Eval return boundary | `echo eval('return 7; echo \"X\";'),'A';` | `7A` |\n| Mutation before exception | `$x=1; try { eval('$x=2; throw new Exception(\"e\");'); } catch(Exception $e){ echo $x; }` | `2` |\n| Parse failure is atomic | `$x=1; try { eval('$x=2; @@@'); } catch(ParseError $e){ echo 'P'; } echo $x;` | `P1` |\n| Declaration persistence | Function `f()` runs `eval('function made(){return 6;} class Made {}');`; after `f()` returns, print `made()` and `class_exists('Made',false)` | `61` |\n| Empty-static-CV cleanup | `class C{function __destruct(){echo 'D';}} function f(){eval('$o=new C;');} f(); echo 'A';` | `DA` |\n| Mixed HTML | `eval('echo \"A\"; ?>B<?php echo \"C\";');` | `ABC` |\n\nAlso author an operand-side-effect fixture proving the source expression runs once, and a closure/reference fixture proving eval sees the closure’s bound local cells. These exercise the same evaluation and ownership paths identified above, rather than a mocked symbol-table implementation.\n\nKeep these oracle-first cases pending without guessing exact diagnostic text or timing:\n\n| Fixture family | What the oracle must settle |\n|---|---|\n| Hoisting | Call before an unconditional function declaration; instantiate before a class declaration; conditional declarations; unavailable parent/interface/trait. |\n| Repeated declarations | Same eval twice; collision with outer declarations/builtins; class/function fatal timing, catchability, and partial declaration visibility. |\n| Method context | `$this`, private access, `self`, `parent`, late-static binding, rebound closures. |\n| Separate compilation environment | Caller namespace and aliases versus eval’s explicit namespace/imports. |\n| Eval-unit statics | Repeated eval and nested eval; lifetime independent of caller function statics. |\n| Source identity | `__FILE__`, `__DIR__`, `__LINE__`, nested eval diagnostic locations and messages. |\n| Operand/encoding | Scalar/null/array/object coercion; UTF-8 source bytes versus the PHP byte-string carrier; invalid byte sequences. |\n| Errors | Duplicate declarations within one eval unit and other compile-time fatals versus runtime guest exceptions. |\n| Generator lifecycle | Eval-created object/reference cleanup on completion, throw, and generator destruction. |\n\nThe encoding fixture is necessary because PHP runtime strings are byte carriers while the current parser accepts a host string and computes UTF-8 offsets. The bridge must avoid double encoding or silently replacing bytes. See [prelude.cljc:19](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:19) and [antlr/cst.clj:123](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj:123).\n\nAdd operational fixtures independently of the PHP oracle:\n\n- Empty eval with budget zero; exhaustion inside an eval loop; repeated/nested eval sharing one counter; calling an eval-declared function after eval returns. Assert the existing fatal text and absence of guest catch/shutdown/destructor output.\n- Missing compiler on JVM, Node, and Dart: the pinned eval refusal, without guest `ParseError`.\n- Configured service on Node/Dart; delayed response; full request stream; duplicate response; cancellation and terminal transport loss. Assert one execution and preserved caller state.\n- Same compiled artifact executed twice: assignments run twice; declarations are checked again.\n- Canonical source and derivation remain retrievable.\n\nThese extend the current safepoint fatal tests and stream-stage retry contract. See [safepoint_test.clj:17](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/php/antlr/safepoint_test.clj:17), [stage.cljc:16](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/stage.cljc:16), and [yang/safepoint.cljc:26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:26).\n\nEval must preserve the existing C5/C6/C11/C12/C13 restrictions. Passing unsupported source through eval must not bypass them, and the bridge must not claim those corners closed. Their existing classifications and P9 refusal fixtures are at [yang.php.md:2216](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:2216), [yang.php.md:2245](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:2245), and [builtin_surfaces_test.clj:18](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/php/antlr/builtin_surfaces_test.clj:18).\n\n**5. Smallest engineer-round plan**\n\nThe plan is independent of the assigned engineer or delegate.\n\n1. **Pin contracts and oracle cases.** Add eval fixtures, request/outcome shapes, eval goal, runtime import inventory, and missing-compiler behavior. Keep the current refusal test until the supported path is available. Start with globals, function locals, return/null, references, syntax failure, and declaration timing.\n2. **Build one JVM vertical path.** Add the eval parser goal and eval-unit emitter through the existing stage pipeline. Publish a code-only entry, retrieve it through the profiled installed-export accessor, and execute it using explicit runtime/scope/context arguments. Prove global write-back and local write-back through a real compiler request.\n3. **Complete ownership and failure handling.** Fix Eval-expression scope recognition, empty-CV cleanup, generator dictionary cleanup, `ParseError` hierarchy, structured operational refusal, and declaration collision handling. The lifecycle and declaration fixtures are gates, not follow-up cleanup.\n4. **Join the run context.** Derive eval sites using caller hook closures; verify one budget across eval, nested eval, and later declared-function calls. Keep compilation limits separate.\n5. **Complete the execution matrix.** Run source-driven JVM tests on all four evaluators, then parserless/service-backed Node and Dart tests, in bundled and linked caller profiles wherever those profiles are claimed. The existing JVM harness already runs four evaluators, but its current budget wrapper covers only the initially supplied AST; runtime artifacts need their own derivation. See [e2e_harness.clj:91](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_harness.clj:91).\n\nThe first vertical slice is a useful engineering checkpoint, **not P9 completion**. Landing support requires the claimed semantic fixtures to be recorded against PHP 8.3.35 and all remaining limitations to be explicitly classified under §17.3.\n\n**6. Draft §3.12 amendment**\n\nReplace the current two-sentence section at [yang.php.md:358](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:358) with:\n\n> `eval` submits a new compilation request through the configured compiler composition (`yang.antlr.md` sections 6.1 and 8.6). The VM does not parse source. The request pins the frontend/compiler profile and eval source goal; parsing begins in PHP mode and retains source spans relative to the supplied eval bytes.\n>\n> Eval executes its compiled unit in the caller’s task. A function containing eval uses the dynamic-scope profile. Its symbol table maps names to the caller’s existing CV cells; eval reads, writes, reference bindings and unset operate on those same cells. New variables enter that table. There is no value snapshot or copy-back step. At global scope the table is `$GLOBALS`. Eval borrows the scope; its owner releases the complete table at function or generator exit.\n>\n> Each eval has its own return boundary. An explicit return supplies eval’s result; falling through or returning without a value yields PHP null. Runtime exceptions propagate through the caller’s handlers, preserving earlier mutations. Invalid eval syntax raises guest `ParseError`. Compiler, transport, resource and admission failures remain operational outcomes.\n>\n> Eval is a separate compilation unit. Its declarations use the normal request-level function and class tables and remain available after eval returns. Hoisting occurs when the eval unit executes; repeated eval does not suppress redeclaration checks. The caller’s variable and method execution context is supplied explicitly; its namespace/import environment is not implicitly copied.\n>\n> Eval invocation and eval-generated loop/call sites use the caller’s active safepoint hooks. Nested eval and functions declared by eval consume the same run budget. Eval never initializes another budget counter. Compiler work and transport waits use separate composition limits.\n>\n> The current local PHP parser is JVM-only. Node and Dart may use an explicitly configured compiler service. Without a configured compiler, eval is refused with `:yang.php/diagnostic :yang.php/unsupported` and `:construct :eval`. Precompiling the enclosing program does not provide runtime eval support. Reused artifacts must match the compilation request and execute at every eval invocation.\n>\n> Eval retains the enclosing frontend’s supported and refused constructs. Its compiler bridge does not close unrelated P9 corners. Pending semantic details require pinned oracle fixtures before support is claimed.\n\nAlso update the P9 status wording to distinguish **builtin surfaces landed; eval bridge pending** until this acceptance gate passes. The row’s existing symbol-table acceptance should remain unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '3680,3890p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc; cat src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/eval_coordinator.cljc; cat test/yang/php/antlr/eval_coordinator_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '3680,3890p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc; cat src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/eval_coordinator.cljc; cat test/yang/php/antlr/eval_coordinator_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"                    {(str/lower-case (ns-prefixed ictx (function-written ctx n))) (refs n)}\n                    {})\n                  (mapcat #(walk ictx %))\n                  (core/rule-kids ctx n)))]\n    (into {} (mapcat #(walk (item-ctx ctx %) %)) (:items ctx))))\n\n\n(def entry-path\n  \"The entry unit's declared path when the composition names none.\"\n  \"main.php\")\n\n\n(defn- context\n  ([packet] (context packet {}))\n  ([packet {:keys [path linked?], :or {path entry-path}}]\n   (let [pk (p/validate! packet {:diagnostic-ns diagnostic-ns})\n         ctx {:pk pk,\n              :analysis (scope/analyze pk),\n              :loops [],\n              :unit {:path path},\n              :linked? (boolean linked?),\n              :warnings (atom []),\n              :ticks (atom 0)}\n         ctx (merge ctx (unit-layout ctx))]\n     (assoc ctx :declared (declared-functions ctx)))))\n\n\n(defn- check-hoisted!\n  \"A top-level function declared twice, or over a builtin, is a compile\n   error.\"\n  [ctx hoisted]\n  (reduce (fn [seen item]\n            (let [f (rule-head ctx item)\n                  written (ns-prefixed (item-ctx ctx item) (function-written ctx f))\n                  lc (str/lower-case written)]\n              (when (or (contains? seen lc) (contains? prelude/builtin-names lc))\n                (compile-error! f (str \"Cannot redeclare \" written \"()\")))\n              (conj seen lc)))\n          #{}\n          hoisted))\n\n\n(defn- class-item?\n  [ctx item]\n  (contains? #{\"classDeclaration\" \"enumDeclaration\"} (:rule (rule-head ctx item))))\n\n\n(defn- unit-body\n  \"The unit's statements as one node, hoisted function declarations first,\n   then the classes that can be declared at that point (PHP hoists a class\n   whose parent, interfaces and traits already exist); a hoisted class is\n   skipped when execution reaches it.\"\n  [ctx]\n  (let [items (:items ctx)\n        hoisted (filterv #(hoisted? ctx %) items)\n        classes (mapv (fn [item]\n                        (let [c (rule-head ctx item)\n                              [_ spec] (class-spec (item-ctx ctx item) c)]\n                          [(:id item) (symbol (str \"%class-\" (:id c))) spec]))\n                      (filter #(class-item? ctx %) items))\n        syms (into {} (map (fn [[id sym _]] [id sym])) classes)\n        in-place (fn [item]\n                   (if-let [sym (get syms (:id item))]\n                     (app* 'php/declare-late! (u/v sym))\n                     (lower (item-ctx ctx item) item)))]\n    (check-hoisted! ctx hoisted)\n    (let [lowered (mapv (fn [item] [item (in-place item)]) items)\n          body (sequence-node\n                 (concat (mapv second (filter (fn [[item _]] (hoisted? ctx item)) lowered))\n                         (mapv (fn [[_ sym _]] (app* 'php/hoist-class! (u/v sym))) classes)\n                         (mapv second (remove (fn [[item _]] (hoisted? ctx item)) lowered))))]\n      (reduce (fn [inner [_ sym spec]] (u/let1 sym (u/lam [] spec) inner))\n              body\n              (reverse classes)))))\n\n\n(defn- unit-returns?\n  \"True when the unit holds a top-level `return` (outside functions and\n   classes), which needs the escape around its body.\"\n  [ctx]\n  (core/contains-rule? ctx\n                       (p/root (:pk ctx))\n                       \"returnStatement\"\n                       #{\"functionDeclaration\" \"lambdaFunctionExpr\" \"classDeclaration\"\n                         \"enumDeclaration\"}))\n\n\n(defn- unit-node\n  \"`unit-body`, inside the `return` escape when the unit has a top-level\n   `return`: the escape answers its value, the sentinel `:php/no-return`\n   when the body falls off its end (plan P6 R4). An included unit's body\n   (`module?`) always answers the sentinel at its end; the entry unit's\n   value is dropped, so its tree is unchanged when it has no `return`.\"\n  ([ctx] (unit-node ctx false))\n  ([ctx module?]\n   (cond\n     (unit-returns? ctx)\n     (app* 'php/call-ec\n           (u/lam ['%ret] (u/then (unit-body (assoc ctx :ret '%ret)) (u/lit :php/no-return))))\n     module? (u/then (unit-body ctx) (u/lit :php/no-return))\n     :else (unit-body ctx))))\n\n\n(defn lower-module-body\n  \"The unit's statements as one node, before the runtime wrapper: what\n   lowering goldens compare. Hoisted function declarations come first.\"\n  [packet]\n  (unit-body (context packet)))\n\n\n(def option-keys\n  \"The options `lower-program` and the lowering stage accept. Request data\n   never flows here: the harness attaches the composition-supplied request\n   stream and composes its sealed reference as `run-main`'s second operand\n   (`:request-node`, an opaque UAST node) at the composition level — the\n   record itself reaches the runtime only when `run-main` consumes it.\"\n  [:prelude :path :paths :class-map :request-node :response-node :diag-node])\n\n\n(defn- path-map-node\n  \"`(php/bind-path-map! {path thunk ...})` for the composition's published\n   paths (plan P6 R3): the computed include's lookup table.\"\n  [paths]\n  (app* 'php/bind-path-map!\n        (reduce (fn [m path] (app* 'assoc m (u/lit path) (delivery-thunk path)))\n                (u/lit {})\n                (sort paths))))\n\n\n(defn lower-program\n  \"`{:ast a :warnings [d ...]}` for one ok packet: the unit body run by\n   `php/run-main` after the prelude, tail calls marked. Its value is\n   `{:php/out s :php/diag [...] :php/uncaught nil-or-{:class :message}}`.\n   `:warnings` are the lowering's warning-severity diagnostics\n   (`:yang.php/unknown-builtin`, `:yang.php/compile-warning`,\n   `:yang.php/computed-include-path`).\n\n   Options: `:prelude` is `:bundled` (default; the prelude tree ahead of the\n   body) or `:linked` (`(require 'yang.call) (require 'php) (php/init!)`, the profile includes\n   need); `:path` the entry unit's declared path (default `entry-path`);\n   `:paths` the normalized paths whose units the composition published,\n   which a computed include resolves against; `:class-map` true requires\n   the published `phpm.$classmap` and binds its classes and delivery thunks\n   (plan P6 R9), which takes the place of `:paths`; `:request-node` is the\n  opaque `run-main` second-operand node the harness composed from the\n  attached request stream (lowering never embeds request data — `run-main`\n  consumes the record from the stream at runtime);\n  `:response-node` and `:diag-node` are the opaque third and fourth\n  operands: the composition-supplied stream references execution emits\n  on. Absent, `run-main` runs with two operands and the cells\n  accumulate.\"\n  ([packet] (lower-program packet {}))\n  ([packet {:keys [prelude paths path class-map request-node response-node diag-node],\n            :or {prelude :bundled}}]\n   (when-not (contains? #{:bundled :linked} prelude)\n     (throw (ex-info \"unknown PHP prelude profile\"\n                     {:yang.php/diagnostic :yang.php/unsupported,\n                      :construct :prelude,\n                      :prelude prelude})))\n   (let [linked? (= :linked prelude)\n         request-node (or request-node (u/sexp->uast :php/null))\n         ctx (context packet {:path (or path entry-path), :linked? linked?})\n         ;; `run-main`'s second operand arrives composed by the harness from\n         ;; the attached request stream: production initialization consumes\n         ;; the record from the stream at runtime and seeds the superglobals,\n         ;; no postwalk rewrite and no request data through lowering involved.\n         ;; With stream nodes `run-main` takes four operands (request,\n         ;; response and diagnostics references); without them it takes two\n         ;; and the cells accumulate.\n         main (if (or response-node diag-node)\n                (app* 'php/run-main (u/lam [] (unit-node ctx)) request-node\n                      (or response-node (u/sexp->uast :php/null))\n                      (or diag-node (u/sexp->uast :php/null)))\n                (app* 'php/run-main (u/lam [] (unit-node ctx)) request-node))]\n     {:ast (u/mark-tails\n             (if linked?\n               (u/seq-nodes (concat [(app* 'require (u/lit 'yang.call))\n                                     (app* 'require (u/lit prelude/module-name))\n                                     (app* 'php/init!)]\n                                    (when (and (seq paths) (not class-map)) [(path-map-node paths)])\n                                    (when class-map\n                                      [(app* 'require (u/lit classmap-module))\n                                       (app* 'php/bind-class-map!\n                                             (u/v (symbol (str classmap-module) \"classes\"))\n                                             (u/v (symbol (str classmap-module) \"paths\")))])\n                                    [main]))\n               (u/then prelude/uast main))),\n      :warnings @(:warnings ctx)})))\n\n\n(defn lower-packet\n  \"The complete program for one ok packet (`lower-program`'s `:ast`).\"\n  ([packet] (lower-packet packet {}))\n  ([packet opts] (:ast (lower-program packet opts))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The module emitter: one PHP unit as the linker module phpm.<mangled-path>\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def module-exports\n  \"The exports of a compiled PHP unit: its spec and body, immutable after\n   install.\"\n  '#{spec body})\n\n\n(defn imported-unit-names\n  \"The normalized paths the unit statically includes through folded literal\n   paths, source order, distinct, the unit's own path aside. A computed path\n   contributes nothing: it is by name at run time (plan P6 R3). A path that\n   cannot normalize is refused as `lower-include` refuses it.\"\n(ns yang.php.antlr.eval-coordinator\n  \"Composition-supplied eval frontend: admit bytes, parse and validate source.\n   This stage produces no executable artifact.\"\n  (:require [yang.antlr.packet :as packet]\n            [yang.php.antlr.cst :as cst]\n            [yang.php.antlr.eval-admission :as admission]\n            [yang.php.antlr.scope :as scope]))\n\n\n(defn make-worker\n  []\n  (cst/make-eval-worker))\n\n\n(defn compile-request\n  \"Prepare one request for later eval-unit emission. Identity includes the\n   complete request (including bytes and caller-supplied revisions/options),\n   the source goal, grammar and admission profile. No caller runtime state is\n   needed by this service.\"\n  [worker request]\n  (let [{:keys [source] :as admitted} (admission/admit-request request)]\n    (if-not source\n      admitted\n      (let [pk (cst/parse-source worker (:yang.compile/unit request) source)\n            identity {:request request\n                      :yang.php/source (:yang.php/source request)\n                      :yang.php.eval/profile-revision admission/profile-revision\n                      :yang.php/source-goal :eval\n                      :yang.php/grammar cst/grammar-id}]\n        (if (:yang.php/not-php pk)\n          {:request request :identity identity :cst pk}\n          (let [validated (packet/validate! pk {:source source})]\n            (cond-> {:request request :identity identity :cst validated}\n              (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome validated))\n              (assoc :scope (scope/analyze validated)))))))))\n(ns yang.php.antlr.eval-coordinator-test\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is]]\n            [dao.host.text :as text]\n            [yang.antlr.packet :as packet]\n            [yang.php.antlr.eval-admission :as admission]\n            [yang.php.antlr.eval-coordinator :as coordinator]))\n\n\n(defn- carrier\n  [s]\n  (text/units->string (map #(bit-and % 255) (text/utf8-encode s))))\n\n\n(deftest admitted-source-and-identity\n  (let [source \"function f() { eval('return 1;'); } ?>é<?php return 2;\"\n        request {:yang.compile/request :r :yang.compile/unit :u\n                 :yang.compile/provenance :p :yang.php/source (carrier source)}\n        result (coordinator/compile-request (coordinator/make-worker) request)]\n    (is (= :yang.cst/ok (get-in result [:cst :yang.cst/outcome])))\n    (is (empty? (packet/conform (:cst result) source)))\n    (is (true? (:dynamic? (first (vals (get-in result [:scope :scopes]))))))\n    (is (= (carrier source) (get-in result [:identity :yang.php/source])))\n    (is (= admission/profile-revision (get-in result [:identity :yang.php.eval/profile-revision])))\n    (is (not= (:identity result)\n              (:identity (coordinator/compile-request (coordinator/make-worker)\n                                                      (assoc request :yang.php/source (carrier (str source \" \")))))))))\n\n\n(deftest malformed-source-is-refused\n  (let [result (coordinator/compile-request (coordinator/make-worker)\n                                            {:yang.compile/request :r :yang.compile/unit :u\n                                             :yang.compile/provenance :p :yang.php/source (text/units->string [255])})]\n    (is (= :yang.compile/rejected (:yang.compile/outcome result)))\n    (is (= :source-encoding (get-in result [:yang.compile/diagnostic :reason])))\n    (is (= :p (:yang.compile/provenance result)))))\n\n\n(deftest original-byte-locations-and-goals\n  (doseq [source [\"\" \"return 'é中😀';\\r\\n?>html<?php echo 1;\" \"// é\\nreturn 1;\"]]\n    (let [request {:yang.compile/unit :u :yang.php/source (carrier source)}\n          result (coordinator/compile-request (coordinator/make-worker) request)\n          pk (:cst result)\n          byte-count (count (text/utf8-encode source))\n          eof (last (filter #(and (= :token (:kind %)) (= \"EOF\" (:type %)))\n                            (:yang.cst/nodes pk)))]\n      (is (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome pk)))\n      (is (empty? (packet/conform pk source)))\n      (is (= [byte-count byte-count] (:span eof)))\n      (is (= (into [0] (keep-indexed (fn [i b] (when (= b 10) (inc i)))\n                                     (text/utf8-encode source)))\n             (:yang.php/line-starts pk)))))\n  (let [request {:yang.compile/unit :u :yang.php/source (carrier \"return 1;\")}\n        worker (coordinator/make-worker)]\n    (is (not= (:identity (coordinator/compile-request worker request))\n              (:identity (coordinator/compile-request worker (assoc request :revision 2)))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
